WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
England, December 30th, 1942.

00:05.000 --> 00:08.000
Dear mother, dad, and gang.

00:08.000 --> 00:11.000
Though I'm not supposed to tell you where I've been,

00:11.000 --> 00:17.000
I think even the hardest of senses would let a guy tell about Christmas Day as I enjoyed it over here.

00:17.000 --> 00:20.000
I have to start with Christmas Eve.

00:20.000 --> 00:23.000
It was then I opened your various Christmas parcels.

00:23.000 --> 00:28.000
Some of the fellows, the rats, were standing around helping and grabbing.

00:28.000 --> 00:30.000
Hey, what are you up to, guys?

00:30.000 --> 00:32.000
This is my cookie. I like chocolate.

00:32.000 --> 00:34.000
Hey, Charlie, your mother's a swell cook.

00:34.000 --> 00:35.000
Good.

00:35.000 --> 00:38.000
They opened this one, Charlie. It's small, but heavy.

00:41.000 --> 00:43.000
Hey, will you look at that.

00:43.000 --> 00:45.000
This is what I've been yelling for. What is it?

00:45.000 --> 00:47.000
Oh, that's a fucking whetstone.

00:47.000 --> 00:49.000
Isn't that swell? My father said to do that.

00:49.000 --> 00:52.000
Now, I don't care whether I can get new rays of plays or not.

00:52.000 --> 00:55.000
You'd better hang on to that brother of Alfie's wife on it.

00:55.000 --> 00:57.000
I can have anything but my whetstone.

00:57.000 --> 00:59.000
I'm going to hang on to it.

00:59.000 --> 01:01.000
Come in.

01:01.000 --> 01:03.000
That'd be so nice of you to do that.

01:13.000 --> 01:15.000
Hey.

01:15.000 --> 01:18.000
I'm going to have dinner with the queen.

01:18.000 --> 01:20.000
She's been out in the sun for a long time.

01:20.000 --> 01:22.000
That's what the note says.

01:22.000 --> 01:24.000
And it's ambrose.

01:24.000 --> 01:27.000
And I have been chosen as the two American officers

01:27.000 --> 01:30.000
to have Christmas dinner with Queen Mary.

01:30.000 --> 01:32.000
Wow.

01:36.000 --> 01:38.000
It wasn't kidding, mother and dad.

01:38.000 --> 01:40.000
We really were invited.

01:40.000 --> 01:42.000
You know, Mother, you've always told me

01:42.000 --> 01:46.000
it was nice to take along some little thing to your hostess.

01:46.000 --> 01:48.000
So thanks for your Christmas presents,

01:48.000 --> 01:51.000
I was able to put together a small package for the queen.

01:52.000 --> 01:56.000
I admit I hesitated some time over what to put in.

01:56.000 --> 01:59.000
And it was hardly a royal-looking parcel.

01:59.000 --> 02:04.000
A box-labeled perfection paper clip, sick gross.

02:04.000 --> 02:07.000
But I somehow thought the queen wouldn't mind.

02:07.000 --> 02:10.000
Well, when we got there...

02:10.000 --> 02:13.000
Your Majesty, Captain Charles Bedley,

02:13.000 --> 02:15.000
Mr. Ensemblish.

02:15.000 --> 02:18.000
Oh, you Captain Bedley, Mr. Ensemblish.

02:18.000 --> 02:20.000
I'm very glad to see you.

02:20.000 --> 02:22.000
How are you doing, Your Majesty?

02:22.000 --> 02:24.000
It was very nice of you to come.

02:24.000 --> 02:26.000
It was nice of us.

02:26.000 --> 02:27.000
Golly.

02:27.000 --> 02:29.000
I think Christmas is the time

02:29.000 --> 02:31.000
when we all want to be with our families.

02:31.000 --> 02:33.000
But if you come,

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
well, the next best thing is to be with someone else.

02:36.000 --> 02:38.000
You see?

02:38.000 --> 02:41.000
My family's broken up by the war, too, you know.

02:41.000 --> 02:43.000
Oh, yes.

02:43.000 --> 02:45.000
Yes, I guess that's the problem.

02:45.000 --> 02:47.000
Now, just be in peace.

02:47.000 --> 02:50.000
Will you accept a little gift from me?

02:50.000 --> 02:52.000
Captain Bedley has one for you

02:52.000 --> 02:55.000
and one for you, Mr. Ensemblish.

02:55.000 --> 02:57.000
Thank you, Your Majesty.

02:57.000 --> 03:00.000
Captain Bedley, I see you have a package.

03:00.000 --> 03:04.000
Could that fly any chance to present to me?

03:04.000 --> 03:06.000
Yes, it is, Your Majesty.

03:06.000 --> 03:08.000
You laugh, I guess.

03:08.000 --> 03:10.000
It isn't really paper clip,

03:10.000 --> 03:12.000
but that's the only box I have.

03:13.000 --> 03:16.000
Let's all open our presents together, shall we?

03:22.000 --> 03:24.000
So, it's a gift, Your Majesty.

03:24.000 --> 03:26.000
You need your test on it, Your Majesty.

03:26.000 --> 03:28.000
I've got a silver pen, knife and a pencil.

03:28.000 --> 03:30.000
Oh, well, I'll hang on to these.

03:30.000 --> 03:33.000
Oh, Captain Bedley, how sweet.

03:33.000 --> 03:36.000
You know how long the chopsticks have been here.

03:36.000 --> 03:39.000
And the soap, don't you?

03:39.000 --> 03:43.000
Don't you tell me about this little thing?

03:43.000 --> 03:45.000
Oh, it's a redstone,

03:45.000 --> 03:48.000
sharp and nice and red, the way it is.

03:48.000 --> 03:50.000
I thought that maybe down in the kitchen

03:50.000 --> 03:52.000
or maybe the king had a reason to teach.

03:52.000 --> 03:55.000
Oh, I'm sure it's a very big need.

03:55.000 --> 03:58.000
I know these things are in your Christmas boxes from home

03:58.000 --> 04:02.000
and I think it's very generous of you to share them with me.

04:02.000 --> 04:03.000
How do you like it?

04:03.000 --> 04:05.000
Join the other guests and go into dinner.

04:05.000 --> 04:09.000
Oh, Captain Bedley, you always fit on my list.

04:09.000 --> 04:13.000
I would like to hear about a list of your family

04:13.000 --> 04:16.000
and all the things you do at home.

04:22.000 --> 04:25.000
When Lieutenant Bresch and I left at midnight,

04:25.000 --> 04:29.000
it was as if we'd spent an evening with old friends.

04:29.000 --> 04:31.000
It was great.

04:31.000 --> 04:33.000
Must close now.

04:33.000 --> 04:35.000
Hope you're all well.

04:35.000 --> 04:38.000
Well, darling.

